    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Original polio history**: Age at onset, muscles affected, extent of initial paralysis, recovery pattern, childhood treatments (bracing, surgery, rehabilitation).\n- **Stable period**: Duration of functional stability after initial recovery. What activities was he able to perform? This "plateau period" typically lasts 25-40+ years before post-polio symptoms emerge.\n- **New symptoms timeline**: Gradual onset of new weakness, fatigue, muscle pain, joint pain. Characterize each symptom.\n- **Fatigue assessment**: Distinguish **central fatigue** (overwhelming exhaustion, cognitive fog) from **peripheral fatigue** (rapid muscle fatigability with use). Both are common in post-polio syndrome (PPS). Use Fatigue Severity Scale.\n- **Pain characterization**: Muscle pain (overuse of compensatory muscles), joint pain (biomechanical stress from chronic asymmetry), or neuropathic pain.\n- **Functional decline**: Specific activities now difficult — stairs, walking distance, transfers, ADLs, work tasks. Quantify decline.\n- **Cold intolerance**: Common in PPS-affected limbs due to autonomic dysfunction.\n- **Respiratory function**: Any dyspnea, sleep-disordered breathing, or morning headaches? Respiratory muscle involvement may have been subclinical initially.\n- **Swallowing**: Bulbar polio involvement? New dysphagia?\n- **Weight changes**: Weight gain from reduced activity worsens biomechanical stress.\n- **Psychological impact**: Depression, grief over loss of hard-won function, identity as a "polio survivor."',
