      content: '**Post-Polio Syndrome Diagnostic Criteria** (March of Dimes criteria):\n1. **Confirmed history of paralytic poliomyelitis** with evidence of motor neuron loss (residual weakness, atrophy, areflexia on exam)\n2. **Period of partial or complete neurologic recovery** followed by a **stable interval** of at least 15 years (typically 25-40 years)\n3. **Gradual or sudden onset** of new progressive and persistent muscle weakness, with or without fatigue, muscle atrophy, or muscle/joint pain\n4. **Symptoms persist for at least 1 year**\n5. **Exclusion of other conditions** that could explain the symptoms\n\n**PPS is a diagnosis of exclusion**. The pathophysiology involves progressive dysfunction and dropout of enlarged motor units that were created during recovery from acute polio (through collateral sprouting and reinnervation). Over decades, these oversized motor units cannot be maintained.\n\n**Differentiating PPS from ALS** (critical distinction):\n\n| Feature | Post-Polio Syndrome | ALS |\n|---|---|---|\n| Progression | Very slow (years) | Rapid (months) |\n| Upper motor neuron signs | Absent | Present (hyperreflexia, Babinski, spasticity) |\n| Sensory findings | Absent | Absent |\n| Fasciculations | May be present (chronic) | Prominent, widespread |\n| Bulbar involvement | Only if prior bulbar polio | Common |\n| Distribution | Muscles affected by original polio and compensatory muscles | Diffuse, not limited to prior polio distribution |\n| Reflexes | Reduced/absent | Pathologically brisk |\n\n**This patient**: No upper motor neuron signs (no hyperreflexia, no Babinski, no spasticity), very slow progression over 2 years, weakness predominantly in prior-polio-affected muscles — consistent with PPS over ALS.\n\n**EMG/NCS Expected Findings in PPS**:\n- **Nerve conduction studies**: Motor amplitudes may be reduced in affected limbs. Sensory studies should be normal (polio does not affect sensory neurons). Normal conduction velocities.\n- **Needle EMG**: \n  - **Chronic denervation-reinnervation changes**: Large-amplitude, long-duration, polyphasic motor unit potentials (MUPs) — evidence of prior collateral sprouting\n  - **Fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves**: Present in actively denervating muscles, indicating ongoing motor neuron dropout\n  - **Reduced recruitment**: Fewer motor units firing at higher rates\n  - **Fasciculation potentials**: May be present but are typically stable, not the rapidly progressive pattern seen in ALS\n  - **Key distinction from ALS**: In PPS, active denervation is confined primarily to muscles in the distribution of original polio involvement (and compensatory muscles). In ALS, active denervation is widespread across multiple myotomes and progresses rapidly.\n\n**Additional Workup**:\n- MRI cervical and lumbar spine — rule out radiculopathy, myelopathy, spinal stenosis\n- TSH, vitamin B12, CK — screen for metabolic and myopathic causes\n- Sleep study — if respiratory symptoms or sleep complaints (sleep-disordered breathing is common)\n- Pulmonary function tests — baseline and serial monitoring',
