      content: '**Core Management Principles for PPS**:\nThe overarching philosophy is **"less is more"** — unlike most rehabilitation paradigms, patients with PPS must reduce physical stress on weakened motor units to preserve function.\n\n**1. Energy Conservation and Pacing**:\n- **Activity modification**: Distribute tasks throughout the day with scheduled rest periods. Use the "50% rule" — do only 50% of what you think you can do before resting.\n- **Pacing education**: Alternate strenuous and light activities. Plan the most demanding tasks for peak energy times (typically morning).\n- **Planned rest periods**: 15-20 minute rest breaks mid-morning and mid-afternoon. Even brief rest periods allow motor unit recovery.\n- **Weight management**: BMI 28 — even modest weight loss reduces biomechanical stress on weakened muscles and joints.\n- **Lifestyle restructuring**: May need to modify work duties, reduce hours, or consider vocational rehabilitation.\n\n**2. Exercise Prescription (Non-Fatiguing)**:\n- **DO prescribe**: Low-intensity, non-fatiguing exercise. Swimming or aquatic therapy (buoyancy unloads joints). Gentle cycling at low resistance. General conditioning at sub-maximal effort.\n- **DO NOT prescribe**: High-intensity strengthening, progressive resistive exercises for severely weakened muscles, or "no pain no gain" approaches. Overuse exercise accelerates motor unit dropout.\n- **Guidelines**: Exercise to the point of mild fatigue, NOT exhaustion. If a muscle is weaker the next day, the exercise was too intense. Allow 48 hours between strengthening sessions for the same muscle group.\n- **Stretching**: Gentle range of motion and stretching daily — safe and beneficial for maintaining flexibility.\n\n**3. Assistive Devices and Orthotics**:\n- **Knee-ankle-foot orthosis (KAFO) or knee orthosis**: Evaluate for a stance-control KAFO or carbon fiber AFO with anterior tibial shell for the left leg — address the genu recurvatum and reduce quadriceps demand.\n- **Cane or forearm crutch**: Left-side weakness warrants assistive device for community ambulation — use a cane in the right hand to unload the left leg.\n- **Motorized scooter or wheelchair**: For longer community distances to conserve energy. Many PPS patients resist this strongly — frame as an "energy conservation tool" that preserves function for priority activities.\n- **Adaptive equipment**: Reacher, shower bench, raised toilet seat, grab bars.\n\n**4. Pain Management**:\n- **Shoulder pain**: Modify compensatory upper extremity weight-bearing. Shoulder strengthening within non-fatiguing limits. Consider subacromial corticosteroid injection for impingement. Avoid overhead activities.\n- **Muscle pain**: Warm compresses, gentle massage, low-dose NSAIDs as needed. Avoid chronic NSAID use.\n- **Joint pain**: Biomechanical optimization with orthoses, weight management, activity modification.\n\n**5. Respiratory Monitoring**:\n- Serial pulmonary function tests every 1-2 years (FVC, MIP, MEP)\n- If FVC drops below 50% predicted or symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation develop, initiate nocturnal non-invasive ventilation (BiPAP)\n- Pneumococcal and influenza vaccination essential\n\n**6. Pharmacology**:\n- No FDA-approved medication for PPS weakness or fatigue. Pyridostigmine has shown modest benefit in some studies. Modafinil may help central fatigue.\n- Avoid medications that worsen neuromuscular transmission (aminoglycosides, high-dose magnesium).',
